### Operator Presentations

#### [2.1 Resilient AI Supercomputer Networking using MRC and SRv6](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-srv6ops-21-resilient-ai-supercomputer-networking-using-mrc-and-srv6-01)
- **Presenter**: Mark Handley (OpenAI)
- **Technical Overview**:
  - OpenAI operates a large SRv6 deployment (approx. 10 million optical links routed with SRv6) for synchronous AI pretraining across clusters exceeding 100,000 GPUs.
  - Objective is eliminating tail latency outliers (100th percentile flow completion times) rather than optimizing mean throughput.
  - Employs a multi-plane 2-tier Claus topology using IPv6-in-IPv6 encapsulation with NEXT-C-SID (uN) micro-SIDs and static routing. No SRH is currently used.
  - Network intelligence is moved to the edge using **MRC** (Multi-Path Reliable Connection), an OCP transport protocol developed with industry partners. Senders spray packets across 256 paths per flow using SRv6 source routing to achieve fine-grained load balancing.
  - BGP is not used for production data traffic path selection (only retained for ICMP echo responses); transport layer handles active load balancing, probing, and sub-RTT link failure detection/rerouting.
- **Q&A and Discussion**:
  - *Yuya (SoftBank)* inquired about ASIC/NOS vendor diversity. Mark clarified that while single sites use a unified NOS/ASIC, the overall network runs across a heterogeneous mix of hardware/ASICs across sites.
  - *Jimmy (Huawei)* asked about candidate path/policy management. Mark clarified that active/backup path selection logic resides entirely within the host NIC (MRC transport protocol layer) using static configuration maps, not on switches or centralized controllers.
  - *Jeff Tantsura (NVIDIA)* asked about controller complexity. Mark noted that switch configurations are kept extremely simple (static routing tables loaded at commissioning).

#### [2.2 SRv6 Powered Backbone Networks](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-srv6ops-22-srv6-powered-backbone-networks-00)
- **Presenter**: Jasmith Baga
- **Technical Overview**:
  - Detailed design and operational experience of building a multi-petabit regional backbone/WAN to interconnect collocated storage and compute regions within a strict 10ms latency bound.
  - Chose shallow buffer data-center class switches (saving power/space over deep buffer WAN switches) combined with custom host congestion control and explicit traffic engineering.
  - Selected SRv6 over MPLS to preserve 6 ECN traffic classes entering/exchanging between data centers and WAN, and to align with the broader SRv6 ASIC ecosystem.
  - Stack uses dynamic traffic engineering per plane (up to 64 planes) with `NEXT-C-SID` (F3216 / USH) and Binding SIDs to overcome hardware hop limits without SRH (due to inconsistent hardware SRH support across vendors).
- **Q&A and Discussion**:
  - *Shraddha Hegde* asked about link vs. node protection and OAM probing. Jasmith noted the controller programs backup paths for protected long-haul links to guarantee latency bounds. Simple SRv6-aware source-routed pings are used for path monitoring.
  - *Jeff Tantsura (NVIDIA)* commented on SID structure sizing (32/16 vs 16/16).

#### [2.3 SRv6 for AI Backend](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-srv6ops-23-srv6-for-ai-backend-01)
- **Presenter**: Abhishek Doshi (Microsoft)
- **Technical Overview**:
  - Discussed Azure's AI backend deployment using SRv6 within SONiC to interconnect massive GPU clusters across buildings/sites.
  - Addressed traffic engineering and fast failover for bursty elephant flows. Uses 32-bit locator + 16-bit uN encoding (up to 6 hops per packet without SRH).
  - Configured statically via central controller down to SONiC `ConfigDB` (`my_locator` and `my_sid` tables), mapping into FRR and SAI/ASIC without relying on BGP control plane complexity.
  - Outlined ongoing efforts in SONiC to expand SRv6 features (uA forwarding, L3VPN, GRT routing table integration).
  - *Related Draft*: `draft-filsfils-srv6ops-srv6-ai-backend` (noted in session chat).

