### Individual Draft Presentations

#### [3.3 Operational Guidance for Protection Mechanisms](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-srv6ops-33-operational-guidance-for-protection-mechanisms-00)
- **Presenter**: Yisong Liu
- **Updates**: Restricted scope strictly to SRv6 data plane (excluding SR-MPLS). Expanded details on egress protection using mirror SIDs, liveness checks, ingress switchover mechanisms, locator visibility considerations, and encapsulation/MSD checks.
- **Status**: Authors requested WG adoption. Chairs noted this document is next in line for an adoption call following the current queue.

#### [3.4 BFD Encapsulation in SRv6 Policy](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-srv6ops-34-bfd-encapsulation-in-srv6-policy-00)
- **Presenter**: Shalvin
- **Technical Details**: Evaluated insert mode vs. encapsulation mode for BFD in SRv6 policies across overhead, legacy hardware support, debugging ease, and greenfield/brownfield applicability. Recommended addressing selections and state explosion mitigations (binding BFD to candidate paths or using Seamless BFD).
- **Feedback**: Chairs noted that the draft needs to focus strictly on operational guidance and trade-offs rather than protocol implementation instructions.

#### [3.5 SRv6-based Rate Control](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-srv6ops-35-srv6-based-rate-control-00)
- **Presenter**: Chen
- **Technical Details**: Proposed operational considerations for dynamic CIR/PIR token-bucket rate enforcement at the SRv6 head-end for oversubscribed network slices.
- **Feedback**: Chairs highlighted that protocol mechanisms for this work are not yet standardized in protocol WGs; operational guidance should be coordinated alongside standard protocol work.

#### [3.6 VXLAN and SRv6 Gateway](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-srv6ops-36-vxlan-and-srv6-gateway-00)
- **Presenter**: Xiaochu
- **Technical Details**: Outlined deployment models for interworking data center VXLAN networks with SRv6 networks: Service Interworking (L3VPN / EVPN translation boundary) vs. VXLAN-over-SRv6 encapsulation.
- **Discussion**: Jeff Tantsura noted that VXLAN-over-SRv6 encapsulated transit involves minimal processing overhead at line rate on modern ASICs, whereas service interworking requires VRF instantiation, decapsulation, and multi-lookup steps.

#### [3.7 SRv6 SFC Deployment Options](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-srv6ops-37-srv6-sfc-deployment-options-00)
- **Presenter**: Jamie
- **Technical Details**: Guidelines for deploying SRv6 Service Function Chaining (SFC) comparing SRv6-aware vs. SRv6-unaware (proxy-assisted) SFs across brownfield and greenfield environments, addressing multi-tenant SID allocation models and protection mechanisms.

